 "drivingQuestion": "Whose view does the mishna's implied liability for vinegar follow?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the mishna follows Rabbi",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yoma.81b.10",
   "daf": "81b",
   "sentenceId": 9,
   "he": "שָׁתָה צִיר אוֹ מוּרְיָיס — פָּטוּר. הָא חוֹמֶץ — חַיָּיב. מַתְנִיתִין מַנִּי? רַבִּי הִיא. דְּתַנְיָא: רַבִּי אוֹמֵר: חוֹמֶץ מֵשִׁיב אֶת הַנֶּפֶשׁ.",
   "en": "It was taught in the mishna that if on Yom Kippur one «drank fish brine or» the briny «liquid» in which fish are pickled, «he is exempt.» The Gemara comments: From the language of the mishna it may be inferred that «if one drank vinegar, he is liable. Who is» the tanna of «the mishna? It is Rabbi» Yehuda HaNasi, «as it was taught» in a baraita that «Rabbi» Yehuda HaNasi «says: Vinegar revives the spirit» and is therefore considered a beverage."
  }
 ],
